 A merge request's description (plan §2 #171–#174; request `docs/slices/request-mr-description.md`, design `docs/slices/briefs/mr-description-design.md` option A, §1.1–§1.6): `description.rs` (the IPC types `DescriptionReply` `read { description }`/`glabMissing`/`notLoggedIn { detail }`/`failed { message }`, `Description { blocks }`, `DescriptionBlock` `paragraph`/`heading { level: HeadingLevel h1–h6 }`/`code { language, text }`/`list { numbering: ListNumbering bullets|numbers { start }, items: DescriptionItem { task: TaskBox none|open|done|inapplicable, blocks } }`/`quote`/`rule`/`table { head, body: DescriptionRow { cells: DescriptionCell { header, align: CellAlign start|center|end, inlines } } }`/`details { open, summary, blocks }`, `DescriptionInline` `text`/`emphasis`/`strong`/`strike`/`code`/`link { link: DescriptionLink }`/`image { image: DescriptionImage, caption }`/`lineBreak`; the shell's `ReadDescription { reply, pages, uploads }`; `read_description`: glab missing → `GlabMissing`, `logged_in_project`, `fetch_description` — the one request, `read_links` reuses it: one `Glab::get` of `description_args` (`MergeRequestDescription { project { mergeRequest(iid) { descriptionHtml } } }`, 10 of 250 logged in, 1.0 s, measured 2026-11-04), `Err` holding glab's line or GitLab's first error, `NO_PROJECT` for `project: null`, `NO_MERGE_REQUEST` for `mergeRequest: null`, `descriptionHtml: null` → "", and `read` makes `failed` of those words — then `described_on` (the one place the walk's base is made: `described` against `WebPage::merge_request`, None off https); one `info` line with iid, outcome, blocks, pages, uploads and web images, never text or an address). **The walk** (`description/html.rs` `described`, `MAX_NESTING` 32): `dom_query` parses the fragment; `Walk::children` is the one place rule 6 is kept — it drops comments, the `LEFT_OUT` elements with all their text (`script`, `style`, `template`, `noscript`, `iframe`, `frame`, `frameset`, `object`, `embed`, `applet`, `video`, `audio`, `source`, `track`, `canvas`, `svg`, `math`, `link`, `meta`, `base`, `head`, `title`, `button`, `select`, `textarea`, `option`, `input`) and every element deeper than 32 (one `warn` per read); block elements `p`, `h1`–`h6`, `pre`, `ul`, `ol`, `blockquote`, `hr`, `table`, `details`, `div` end the inline run before them, which becomes a paragraph unless it is only white space; inline elements `em`/`i`, `strong`/`b`, `del`/`s`/`strike`, `code`, `a`, `img`, `br`; every other element gives its content in place (`span`, `gl-emoji`, `copy-code`, `section`, `form` …); `li`, `thead`/`tbody`/`tfoot`, `tr`, `th`/`td` and `summary` count only inside their parents; a link without `href`, inside another link or left with nothing but white space gives its content only, and its images follow it (`lifted`), so a link never holds an image; an image's address is `data-src` (GitLab's lazy images) else `src`, its caption alt, else title, else the address as written; pages and uploads are collected once each in document order, and `commit_titles` keeps GitLab's non-empty `title` of each `commit` or `commit_range` reference link by its page (the first per page; no other link's `title`). `description/element.rs` reads what one element says: the code language (`data-canonical-lang`, else `lang`, empty → none; the `<pre>`'s text with one final `\n` removed), `<ol start>` (else 1), the heading level, the task box (the item's first content, or its first paragraph's, an `input.task-list-item-checkbox`: `data-inapplicable` → inapplicable, `checked` → done, else open), the cell alignment (exactly `text-align: center`/`right`, else start) and a link's commit title (`commit_title`). **Addresses** (`description/address.rs`: the IPC types `DescriptionLink` `page { page }`/`refused { address }`, `DescriptionImage` `uploaded { key }`/`web { address }`/`refused` and `UploadKey`, and the shell's `OfferedUpload { key, address }`, re-exported from `description.rs`; the only maker of `UploadKey` and, beside `avatar_source`, of `PictureAddress`; `UploadKey::is_key` is the shell's test of a key, `upload~` and the 32 lowercase hex secret): `WebPage::linked` (an `http`/`https` `url::Url` as `url` serialises it: no space, quote, `<`, `>` or control character), `link_of` (`Url::options().base_url(base).parse(href)`; http/https → `page`, else `refused { address: href }`), `image_of` (an empty address or anything but https → `refused`; https on `base`'s origin, only when that origin is exactly `https://gitlab.com`, with the path `/-/project/<digits>/uploads/<32 lowercase hex>/<file>` or `<the merge request's project web path>/uploads/<secret>/<file>`, `<file>` one segment of `[A-Za-z0-9._~%+-]`, not `..` → `uploaded { key: "upload~<secret>" }` with the route `projects/<digits, or the web path with "/" as "%2F">/uploads/<secret>/<file>` and the address without query or fragment; any other https → `web { address }`, an upload on a self-managed GitLab included: gitlab.com was measured to answer both upload routes itself (200, no redirect, 2026-11-04), while a self-managed GitLab may redirect an upload to object storage and glab would carry the login there (RK53), so the webview loads it without a login and it counts as a web image). **Uploads** (`picture.rs`): `PictureAddress` holds an avatar or an upload (route and web address), `as_str` the https address; `fetch_picture` asks an upload by `glab api <route>`, then, only when glab's line ends `(HTTP 403)` or `(HTTP 404)` (`page.rs` `FORBIDDEN`, `NOT_FOUND`), by `glab api <web address>` (both answered gitlab-org/gitlab !200000's screenshots, 62–158 KB PNGs, on 2026-11-04); a 3xx glab did not follow (`glab: HTTP 3xx`) fails on either route and never tries the other: glab 1.126.0 follows a GET's redirect itself, and gitlab.com answered both routes 200 with no redirect (`glab api --include`, 2026-11-04); an upload may be up to `MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES` 10 MiB, an avatar still 1 MiB. The list query asks `description` after `title` (172 of 250 logged in, +2), and `page.rs` folds it to `MergeRequestRow.hasDescription` (more than white space); the text goes nowhere else. Tests: `description/tests.rs` over the measured GitLab shapes and the planted answer of the scenario `mr-description` (its project on gitlab.com), `picture/tests.rs` for both routes and the two limits, `tests/description.rs` over the fake glab (`tests/glab/README.md`; !53 a redirect on either route refused; `mr-description/self-managed` uploads on a self-managed GitLab never fetched).
 
-A merge request's linked epics, issues and commits (plan §2 #175, #177 proposed; request `docs/slices/request-mr-links.md` rules 2–6, design `docs/slices/briefs/mr-links-design.md` option A, §1.1–§1.4, §1.6, §1.7; RK41 amended, RK68, RK69): `links.rs` (the IPC types `LinksReply` `read { links }`/`glabMissing`/`notLoggedIn { detail }`/`failed { message }`, `DescriptionLinks { epics, issues, commits }`, `LinkedEpic { reference, page, shown: ShownEpic { title, closed } | null }`, `LinkedIssue { reference, page, shown: ShownIssue { title, closed, parent: LinkedParent { reference, title, page } | null } | null }`, `LinkedCommit { reference, page, title | null }`; the shell's `ReadLinks { reply, pages }`, `pages` every line's page and each parent's after its issue, once each, empty unless `read`; `read_links`: glab missing → `GlabMissing`, `logged_in_project`, the description's own `fetch_description`, `described_on`, then the lookup only when an issue or epic is linked; one `info` line with iid, outcome, epics, issues, commits, not shown, calls and the highest score, never a title, reference or address). **What counts** (`links/target.rs`, the one reader of an address as a thing): the walk's `pages`, read by `target_of` against the `Server` (the `url::Origin` parsed from `Project.web_origin` and the sub-path, `web_path` without `/<full_path>`; none off https, so nothing is listed and nothing asked): the same `url::Origin`, compared as `description/address.rs` does (a `web_url` with an upper-case host or an explicit `:443` keeps every link), under the sub-path, every segment `is_path_segment`, split at the first `/-/`: `groups/<g…>/-/epics|work_items/<n>` → a group's item, `<p…>/-/issues/<n>`, `issues/incident/<n>`, `work_items/<n>` → a project's item (`p` never `groups`), `<p…>/-/commit/<7–40 lowercase hex>` and `<p…>/-/merge_requests/<n>/diffs?commit_id=<40 hex>` → a commit, `<p…>/-/compare/<sha>...<sha>` (or `..`) → a range, anything else none (query and fragment ignored but `commit_id`); one entry per thing at its first page (`Target::is_same`: a commit whose id starts the other's in the same project is that commit). `reference` writes GitLab's `to_reference` from the project: `#n`, `<sibling>#n`, `<path>#n`; an epic `&n` in the project's namespace, else `<group>&n`; a group's other item `<group>#n`; a commit's first 8 characters after `<path>@` or `<sibling>@`; a range `<from 8>…<to 8>`. **The lookup** (`links/lookup.rs`, `query.rs` `links_args`): `calls` groups the items' iids by namespace in order of first mention, cuts each at `MAX_IIDS_PER_PART` 100 (GitLab's `workItems(iids:)` maximum) and packs the parts in order into calls whose estimate `PART_SCORE` 25 × parts + iids stays ≤ `LOOKUP_BUDGET` 200 (GitLab's limit is 250 logged in); each call is one `Glab::get` of `api graphql -f query=<MergeRequestLinks: queryComplexity, part<k>: project|group(fullPath: $path<k>) { workItems(iids: $iids<k>, first: $first<k>) { nodes { ...linked } } }> -f path<k>=… -F iids<k>=<JSON strings> -F first<k>=<count>`, the fragment asking `iid title state webUrl workItemType { name }` and the hierarchy widget's `parent { iid title webUrl workItemType { name } }` only (fields every edition has at 15.6, `Group.workItems` at 16.3; never `epic`, `epics`, `hasEpic`, `hasParent`, `onlyTypes`, `reference`, `namespace` or `project` on a work item); measured logged in with glab 1.126.0 on gitlab.com 2026-11-04: 50 of 250 for 3 project iids and 2 group iids (6.2 s, the `gitlab-org` group part alone 6.9 s), 124 for 100 project iids (1.6 s). glab's `Err` or a GraphQL `errors` entry fails the reply in those words and no later call runs; `part<k>: null` or an iid absent from `nodes` is not shown, never an error. A node typed `Epic` is an epic (its own parent dropped), any other an issue whose parent is kept only when its `webUrl` reads as an item on the project's server (`WebPage::linked` of it is the page). Grouping: shown epic → Epics, shown other → Issues, not shown → Epics for a group's item, Issues for a project's; commits and ranges → Commits, titled from the walk's `commit_titles` of their first page, never looked up. Tests: `links/tests.rs` (routes, sub-path, an upper-case host or `:443` in the `web_url`, references, one entry per thing, groups, unseen parts, parents, packing, the query's fields, pages, no https), `description/tests.rs` (`commit_titles`), `tests/links.rs` over the fake glab scenario `mr-links` (`tests/glab/README.md`; the route `graphql-links-<iids0>`).
